WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:15.660
So far, I've been telling you about the theory
of QM and how it answers questions about reality.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.490
Today we're going to do something different,
and actually question quantum mechanics itself.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:24.310
I've been looking forward to this.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:28.370
Today we're going to understand a paradox
Einstein helped create to disprove Quantum

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:29.370
mechanics.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:36.380
But first, we need to look at an essential
ingredient in Quantum mechanics, entanglement.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.360
Entanglement isn't something limited to quantum.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.120
Consider this example.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.379
You have two objects, one red, one white.

00:00:44.379 --> 00:00:47.250
You put them into boxes and shuffle them around.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:51.749
Now if you open one of the boxes, and see
it's red, then you know with out having to

00:00:51.749 --> 00:00:55.230
check that the other box has the white object
in it.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:01:00.170
So we say that these two objects are entangled,
which mean knowing something about one causes

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:03.999
you to know something about the other, with
ever having to see look at it.

00:01:03.999 --> 00:01:07.900
However, quantum entanglement is much more
interesting than this garden variety.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.810
Of course the culprit, as it always is in
quantum mechanics, is superposition.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.680
Let's go back to our example.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.760
While the boxes are closed, we don't know
which state the object in each box is in,

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.280
red or white.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.390
If it were truly impossible to know which
one is which -which isn't actually the case

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:29.600
in everyday life, but let's say it was this
time, then quantum mechanics tells us, that

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.070
each object must be both red and white at
the same time.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:35.990
But its even weirder than that.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:41.120
There are only two possibilities, ever, the
A is red, and B is white, or the other way

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.330
around.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:47.070
But superposition tells us, that all possible
things happen at once, so the state of the

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:53.740
two objects is both A is red, B is white and
A is white, B is red.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.690
This is a bizarre superposition.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:59.280
What happens if Alice checks her box?

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:05.130
She collapses the state of A, which is weird
but as usual, but she also collapses the state

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:08.210
of B, which she didn't even touch.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:11.640
I'm sure a lot of you are skeptical at this
point.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.769
Why should we accept the quantum picture,
when its' obvious that the classical version

00:02:15.769 --> 00:02:20.219
has the same results but by a way less convoluted
method?

00:02:20.219 --> 00:02:24.549
Couldn't it be the case that objects aren't
actually in super positions at all?

00:02:24.549 --> 00:02:28.599
This is a very good concern, and one we'll
be taking seriously.

00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:31.569
We're going to try build a test.

00:02:31.569 --> 00:02:33.980
First let's notice something.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:39.569
In quantum mechanics, these particles are
not independent of each other in a deep sense.

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:44.069
You could argue that the classical particles
aren't either, but here's the difference.

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:49.379
In the classical case, measuring one of the
particles, say finding out that its red, doesn't

00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:51.689
actually turn the other one white.

00:02:51.689 --> 00:02:54.019
In fact it does nothing to the other one.

00:02:54.019 --> 00:02:57.010
The objects already had a colour all along.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:01.930
On the other hand, according to quantum mechanics,
the objects have to affect each other.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:07.069
Otherwise, each object is in a superposition
of red and white, and Alice could measure

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:12.219
hers and find it's white, but this doesn't
do anything to B's superposition, so Bob could

00:03:12.219 --> 00:03:15.120
measure his, and might find its white as well!

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.749
But this never happens.

00:03:17.749 --> 00:03:22.329
The EPR paradox is a brilliant and simple
argument that exploits this difference between

00:03:22.329 --> 00:03:24.700
the classical case and the quantum one.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:33.879
They wanted to show that quantum mechanics
can't explain these results.

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:38.700
It's a proof by contradiction, which means
you assume something, in this case, quantum

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:47.799
mechanics, and then show that that would lead
to something happening which is impossible.

00:03:47.799 --> 00:03:51.430
Actually we don't need to assume all of the
quantum mechanics is true- just one little

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:52.680
fact.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.930
We just need to assume that if we have an
entangled pair A and B, then measuring A really

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.999
does affect B.

00:03:59.999 --> 00:04:07.499
We saw that QM has this quality, but we don't
need to worry about superposition etc, for

00:04:07.499 --> 00:04:08.499
this argument.

00:04:08.499 --> 00:04:10.159
Just this fact alone.

00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:16.169
Ok, so now, say that our boxes get shipped
to the other sides of the universe to Alice

00:04:16.169 --> 00:04:17.670
and Bob.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:24.440
Alice opens hers a split second before Bob
opens his Alice finds her object is red, so

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.130
bob must find that his is white.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:31.270
But let's think about what must happen in
that split second between Alice unbox her

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.300
object and Bob doing the same.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.949
A must tell B that its been measured and what
result it decided to be or else B won't know

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:40.460
what to do.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:44.720
But that message has to go an awful long way
in a very short time.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.930
In fact even light couldn't go that.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.060
This is a big problem.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:54.720
If you've heard of Einstein's theory of relativity,
you might know that the fact that 'nothing

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.570
can go faster than the speed of light' is
the foundation of that whole theory.

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:04.639
Therefore accepting our assumption that A
and B can really influence each other is equivalent

00:05:04.639 --> 00:05:07.830
to pulling the rug from underneath relativity.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:13.280
To Einstein, this was impossible so he concluded
that the assumption must be wrong.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:20.510
What does it mean if the assumption is wrong?

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:23.919
Well, that definitely means quantum mechanics
is wrong.

00:05:23.919 --> 00:05:26.440
But actually its even stronger than that.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:31.690
It says that no theory were measuring one
object changes another object can ever be

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:32.700
true.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.020
But then how can we explain that A and B always
have opposite colours?

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.669
We must do it the way classical mechanics
did.

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:45.500
I.e., none of that quantum weirdness, no superposition,
no randomness, no uncertainty principle.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:46.800
This is big.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.380
The conclusion is basically; either we must
believe that 'nothing can go faster than the

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:58.229
speed of light' and accept that quantum mechanics
is completely false, OR we accept Quantum

00:05:58.229 --> 00:06:00.590
but brake the theory of Relativity.

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:01.349
We can't have both.

